.TH "HTMLIMAGEELEMENT.SIZES" "3JS" "May 29, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLImageElement.sizes \- HTMLImageElement: sizes property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]sizes\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]HTMLImageElement\f[R] interface allows you to specify the layout
width of the image for each of a list of media queries, or
\f[CR]auto\f[R] for lazy\-loaded images to allow the browser to
automatically select an image to display based on the layout size of the
element.
This allows the browser to choose between different images specified in
the element \f[CR]srcset\f[R] to match different media conditions \(em
even images with different orientations or aspect ratios.
.PP
The \f[CR]sizes\f[R] property reflects the \f[CR]<img>\f[R] element\(cqs
\f[CR]sizes\f[R] content attribute.
It can only be present when \f[CR]srcset\f[R] uses width descriptors.
.SH VALUE
A string that can be the \f[CR]auto\f[R] keyword (optionally followed by
any number of \f[I]source sizes\f[R]), or one or more \f[I]source
sizes\f[R].
.PP
See the \f[CR]sizes\f[R] attribute in the HTML \f[CR]<img>\f[R]
reference for more information.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Selecting an image to fit window width
This example demonstrates how the browser uses the \f[CR]sizes\f[R]
attribute to select an image from \f[CR]srcset\f[R] based on the
rendered width of the image at the current viewport width.
It also allows you to see the effect of resizing the browser window on
which image is loaded.
.SS HTML
In order to demonstrate the effect of lazy loading the images need to be
initially hidden from the visual viewport, and then scrolled into view.
This is achieved by having an outer \f[CR]scroll\-container\f[R]
\f[CR]<div>\f[R] that nests \f[CR]spacer\f[R] and \f[CR]demo\-wrap\f[R]
containers.
The image is contained inside the \f[CR]demo\-wrap\f[R] container, which
is pushed out of the visual viewport by the height set on the
\f[CR]spacer\f[R] container.
.PP
The \f[CR]<img>\f[R] element has the following attributes:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]srcset\f[R] defines four images and indicates that they are
\f[CR]600px\f[R], \f[CR]900px\f[R], \f[CR]1200px\f[R], and
\f[CR]1500px\f[R] wide.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]src\f[R] specifies the image that will be used if
\f[CR]srcset\f[R] is not supported or it can\(cqt be parsed.
We use the largest image in the \f[CR]srcset\f[R] as this will almost
always downscale better than the smallest image will upscale.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]loading\f[R] is \f[CR]lazy\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]sizes\f[R] specifies the expected rendered width of the image at a
set of viewport\-width breakpoints, allowing the browser to select the
most appropriate image from \f[CR]srcset\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqscroll\-container\(dq>
  Scroll down to display images
  <\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqspacer\(dq></\f[B]div\f[R]>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqdemo\-wrap\(dq>
    <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-container\(dq id=\(dqresizable\(dq>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-square\(dq>
        <\f[B]img\f[R]
          loading=\(dqlazy\(dq
          sizes=\(dq(max\-width: 600px) 600px, (max\-width: 900px) 900px, (max\-width: 1200px) 1200px, 1500px\(dq
          src=\(dq1500.png\(dq
          srcset=\(dq600.png 600w, 900.png 900w, 1200.png 1200w, 1500.png 1500w\(dq
          alt=\(dqExample image\(dq />
      </\f[B]div\f[R]>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqlabel\(dq>
        <\f[B]strong\f[R]>Container width: <\f[B]span\f[R] id=\(dqwidth\-label\(dq></\f[B]span\f[R]></\f[B]strong\f[R]>
      </\f[B]div\f[R]>
    </\f[B]div\f[R]>
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
The CSS and JavaScript are not shown (if you want to examine these,
select \(lqPlay\(rq to view the whole example in the interactive
playground).
.SS Result
The example is best viewed in its own window, so you can adjust the
sizes fully, and the example is not constrained by its containing frame.
.IP "1." 3
Scroll the frame to display the image.
The label at the bottom of the image shows the current container width.
.IP "2." 3
Resize the window \(em you should see the image change at the
\f[CR]sizes\f[R] attribute\(cqs media query break points.
.RS 4
.PP
Note that the selected image may be larger than the container width
alone suggests.
Many displays, if not most, have a device pixel ratio (DPR) greater than
one.
In order to render a sharp image at the physical pixel density of the
display, a browser will multiply the matched \f[CR]sizes\f[R] hint by
the DPR before selecting from \f[CR]srcset\f[R].
For example, on a 2× display with a viewport of \(ti500px, the matched
hint is \f[CR]600px\f[R], but the browser looks for a \(ti1200px image
and selects \f[CR]1200.png\f[R] as the closest available size and then
scales it to fit in the available space.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] As a result, some of the images in the \f[CR]srcset\f[R]
may not be reachable on a particular display at some break points, and
this may be browser dependent.
.RE
.RE
.PP
The log provides information when a \f[CR]load\f[R] event fires for the
image, and when it intersects the visible viewport.
Note that the image is lazy\-loaded, so the \f[CR]load\f[R] event should
be fired just before the image enters the viewport.
.SS Automatic image selection for lazy loaded images
This example demonstrates how setting the \f[CR]sizes\f[R] value to
\f[CR]auto\f[R] affects the selection of the image to load from the
\f[CR]srcset\f[R] when \f[CR]<img>\f[R] elements are lazy\-loaded.
It also allows you to see the effect of changing the size of a container
on the loaded image.
.SS HTML
The HTML is similar to that in the previous example, except that it
defines three near\-identical \f[CR]<img>\f[R] elements, each with a
\f[CR]srcset\f[R] indicating 3 images that are \f[CR]600px\f[R],
\f[CR]400px\f[R], and \f[CR]200px\f[R] wide, and with a \f[CR]sizes\f[R]
value of \f[CR]auto\f[R].
These are constrained within containers that are sized to select the
different images.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqscroll\-container\(dq>
  Scroll down to display images
  <\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqspacer\(dq></\f[B]div\f[R]>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqdemo\-wrap\(dq>
    <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-container img\-container\-\-sm\(dq id=\(dqresizable\(dq>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-square\(dq>
        <\f[B]img\f[R]
          loading=\(dqlazy\(dq
          sizes=\(dqauto\(dq
          src=\(dq600.png\(dq
          srcset=\(dq600.png 600w, 400.png 400w, 200.png 200w\(dq
          alt=\(dqImage in small container\(dq />
      </\f[B]div\f[R]>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqlabel\(dq><\f[B]strong\f[R]>Container width: 100px</\f[B]strong\f[R]></\f[B]div\f[R]>
    </\f[B]div\f[R]>

    <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-container img\-container\-\-md\(dq>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-square\(dq>
        <\f[B]img\f[R]
          loading=\(dqlazy\(dq
          sizes=\(dqauto\(dq
          src=\(dq600.png\(dq
          srcset=\(dq600.png 600w, 400.png 400w, 200.png 200w\(dq
          alt=\(dqImage in medium container\(dq />
      </\f[B]div\f[R]>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqlabel\(dq><\f[B]strong\f[R]>Container width: 200px</\f[B]strong\f[R]></\f[B]div\f[R]>
    </\f[B]div\f[R]>

    <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-container img\-container\-\-lg\(dq>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqimg\-square\(dq>
        <\f[B]img\f[R]
          loading=\(dqlazy\(dq
          sizes=\(dqauto\(dq
          src=\(dq600.png\(dq
          srcset=\(dq600.png 600w, 400.png 400w, 200.png 200w\(dq
          alt=\(dqImage in large container\(dq />
      </\f[B]div\f[R]>
      <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqlabel\(dq><\f[B]strong\f[R]>Container width: 400px</\f[B]strong\f[R]></\f[B]div\f[R]>
    </\f[B]div\f[R]>
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
Here we show the CSS classes that set the size of the different image
containers.
.IP
.EX
\&.img\-container\-\-sm {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: 100px;
}
\&.img\-container\-\-md {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: 200px;
}
\&.img\-container\-\-lg {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: 400px;
}
.EE
.PP
The remaining CSS and the JavaScript that powers the slider, logging,
and so on, are not shown (if you are interested in examining these,
select \(lqPlay\(rq to view the whole example in the interactive
playground).
.SS Result
Scroll the frame to display the three images.
The browser should have selected a different image for each based on the
different width constraints.
You can use the slider to modify the size of the container for the first
image.
Note that the browser may or may not select a new image to display as
the size of the container changes as implementations are not required to
react to dynamic changes.
.PP
The log provides information when a \f[CR]load\f[R] event fires for each
image, and when an image intersects the visible viewport.
Note that the images are lazy\-loaded, so the \f[CR]load\f[R] event
should be fired just before the image enters the viewport.
Also note that the \f[CR]load\f[R] event also fires as you modify the
container size for the first image, indicating when the browser has
recalculated the layout (not necessarily that a new image has been
loaded).
.SS Blog example
This example is a slightly more real\-world case demonstrating how to
select an image to fit a window width using source sizes.
.PP
In this example, a blog\-like layout is created, displaying some text
and an image for which three size points are specified, depending on the
width of the window.
Three versions of the image are also available, with their widths
specified.
The browser takes all of this information and selects an image and width
that best meets the specified values.
.PP
How exactly the images are used may depend upon the browser and the
pixel density of the user\(cqs display.
.PP
Buttons at the bottom of the example let you actually modify the
\f[CR]sizes\f[R] property slightly, switching the largest of the three
widths for the image between 40em and 50em.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]article\f[R]>
  <\f[B]h1\f[R]>An amazing headline</\f[B]h1\f[R]>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqtest\(dq></\f[B]div\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>
    This is even more amazing content text. It\(aqs really spectacular. And
    fascinating. Oh, it\(aqs also clever and witty. Award\-winning stuff, I\(aqm sure.
  </\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]img\f[R]
    src=\(dqnew\-york\-skyline\-wide.jpg\(dq
    srcset=\(dq
      new\-york\-skyline\-wide.jpg 3724w,
      new\-york\-skyline\-4by3.jpg 1961w,
      new\-york\-skyline\-tall.jpg 1060w
    \(dq
    sizes=\(dq(50em \f[B]<\f[R]= width \f[B]<\f[R]= 60em) 50em,
              (40em \f[B]<\f[R]= width \f[B]<\f[R] 50em) 30em,
              (width \f[B]<\f[R] 40em) 20em\(dq
    alt=\(dqThe New York City skyline on a beautiful day, with the One World Trade Center building in the middle.\(dq />
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>
    Then there\(aqs even more amazing stuff to say down here. Can you believe it? I
    sure can\(aqt.
  </\f[B]p\f[R]>

  <\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqbreak40\(dq>Last Width: 40em</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqbreak50\(dq>Last Width: 50em</\f[B]button\f[R]>
</\f[B]article\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
.IP
.EX
article {
  \f[B]margin\f[R]: 1em;
  \f[B]max\-width\f[R]: 60em;
  \f[B]min\-width\f[R]: 20em;
  \f[B]border\f[R]: 4em solid #880e4f;
  \f[B]border\-radius\f[R]: 7em;
  \f[B]padding\f[R]: 1.5em;
  \f[B]font\f[R]:
    16px \(dqOpen Sans\(dq,
    \(dqVerdana\(dq,
    \(dqHelvetica\(dq,
    \(dqArial\(dq,
    sans\-serif;
}

article img {
  \f[B]display\f[R]: block;
  \f[B]max\-width\f[R]: 100%;
  \f[B]border\f[R]: 1px solid #888888;
  \f[B]box\-shadow\f[R]: 0 0.5em 0.3em #888888;
  \f[B]margin\-bottom\f[R]: 1.25em;
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The JavaScript code handles the two buttons that let you toggle the
third width option between 40em and 50em; this is done by handling the
\f[CR]click\f[R] event, using the JavaScript string \f[CR]replace()\f[R]
method to replace the relevant portion of the \f[CR]sizes\f[R] string.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] image = document.querySelector(\(dqarticle img\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] break40 = document.getElementById(\(dqbreak40\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] break50 = document.getElementById(\(dqbreak50\(dq);

break40.addEventListener(
  \(dqclick\(dq,
  () \f[B]=>\f[R] (image.sizes = image.sizes.replace(/50em,/, \(dq40em,\(dq)),
);

break50.addEventListener(
  \(dqclick\(dq,
  () \f[B]=>\f[R] (image.sizes = image.sizes.replace(/40em,/, \(dq50em,\(dq)),
);
.EE
.SS Result
The page is best viewed in its own window, so you can adjust the sizes
fully, and the example is not constrained by its containing frame.
.IP "1." 3
Enable the developer tools and change the width of the page \(em you
should see the image change (and jump in size) at the sizes media query
break points: \f[CR]640px\f[R] (\f[CR]40em\f[R]), and \f[CR]800px\f[R]
(\f[CR]50em\f[R]).
.IP "2." 3
Set the width between \f[CR]50em\f[R] \f[CR](800px)\f[R] and
\f[CR]60em\f[R] (\f[CR]960px\f[R]) so that the last media query is
selected.
Then alternately press each of the buttons and note how the layout size
of the image is changed.
